P(f'Missed medication was the dominant modifiable factor: removing it raised seizure freedom by {ppci(r_miss)} pp and lowered seizures by {abs(r_miss.d_seiz_obs):.2f} per person-year. Stress ({ppci(r_stress)} pp), catamenial timing ({ppci(r_cat)}), illness ({ppci(r_ill)}), binge drinking ({ppci(r_binge)}) and sleep loss ({ppci(r_sleep)}) each mattered a little; sensory overload and photic stimulation were near zero. Removing every trigger raised seizure freedom by {ppci(r_all)} pp, from {pc(b.p_sf12)} to {pc(S.loc["triggers_NONE"].p_sf12)}, and seizures fell from {b.seiz_per_yr:.2f} to {S.loc["triggers_NONE"].seiz_per_yr:.2f} per person-year. Doubling exposure gave roughly mirror effects: missed medication {ppci(con("miss_AMPx2"))} pp (+{con("miss_AMPx2").d_seiz_obs:.2f} seizures per person-year) and stress {ppci(con("stress_AMPx2"))} pp (+{con("stress_AMPx2").d_seiz_obs:.2f}).')
P(f'Triggers were not additive, an emergent result. Summing the effect of removing each trigger alone gives {100 * sum_off:.1f} pp, summing the effect of adding each alone to a trigger-free cohort gives {100 * sum_only:.1f} pp, and the all-off contrast ({100 * r_all.d_sf12:.1f} pp) lies between the two. The cause is positive interaction through the HPA cascades (stress → sleep loss; illness or binge → missed dose) and the shared recruitment gate, so the cost of a trigger depends on what else is present (Section 3.8).')
